      {
        "line_id": null,
        "text": ""
      },
      {
        "line_id": "L001",
        "text": "- Core framing: AI’s only goal is to promote human interests; it is uncertain what those interests are and must infer them"
      },
      {
        "line_id": "L002",
        "text": "- “Interest” preferred over “preference” or “values”:"
      },
      {
        "line_id": "L003",
        "text": "  - “Values” triggered culture-war reactions"
      },
      {
        "line_id": "L004",
        "text": "  - “Stated preferences” are easily manipulated; “interest” implicitly acknowledges that preferences can be shaped against one’s own good"
      },
      {
        "line_id": "L005",
        "text": "- Inference mechanism: observe behavior and internal mental states, apply Bayesian priors"
      },
      {
        "line_id": "L006",
        "text": "- Convergence: in theory, infinite data + fixed model of human decision-making → recover true preferences; in practice, unlikely to fully converge"
      },
      {
        "line_id": "L007",
        "text": "- Non-identifiability problem: behavior alone can’t distinguish preferences from beliefs (e.g., a chess-player example: anti-rational agent looks identical from outside)"
      },
      {
        "line_id": "L008",
        "text": "  - Bayesian prior that someone is anti-rational is infinitesimally low, so practically resolvable"
      },
      {
        "line_id": null,
        "text": ""
      },
      {
        "line_id": null,
        "text": "### Hard Problems for the Assistance Game"
      },
      {
        "line_id": null,
        "text": ""
      },
      {
        "line_id": "L009",
        "text": "- Preferences vs. interests: what someone prefers may not be good for them"
      },
      {
        "line_id": "L010",
        "text": "- Preference plasticity and manipulation:"
      },
      {
        "line_id": "L011",
        "text": "  - Addiction changes preference structures; AI should weight “reflective” preferences over drug-modified ones"
      },
      {
        "line_id": "L012",
        "text": "  - Broader issue: preferences are shaped by external parties (e.g., patriarchal societies shaping women’s preferences)"
      },
      {
        "line_id": "L013",
        "text": "  - Risk: AI systems are now themselves engaged in preference engineering"
      },
      {
        "line_id": "L014",
        "text": "- Aggregation and social choice: what should AI do when serving multiple humans with conflicting interests?"
      },
      {
        "line_id": "L015",
        "text": "- Autonomy dimension:"
      },
      {
        "line_id": "L016",
        "text": "  - Climbing Everest vs. being helicoptered to the top: outcomes matter, but so does agency"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "source_id": "P6641881a8987",
    "path": "vault/raw/alignment-fundamentals-notes.md",
    "lines": [
      {
        "line_id": null,
        "text": ""
      },
      {
        "line_id": null,
        "text": "### AI Safety Course: Group Image Exercise"
      },
      {
        "line_id": null,
        "text": ""
      },
      {
        "line_id": "L017",
        "text": "- Three groups presented AI futures via generated images"
      },
      {
        "line_id": "L018",
        "text": "- Group 1 (Outgroup): framed AI development as “camps”"
      },
      {
        "line_id": "L019",
        "text": "  - Camp 1: slow development"
      },
      {
        "line_id": "L020",
        "text": "  - Camp 2: race ahead (current reality)"
      },
      {
        "line_id": "L021",
        "text": "  - Camp 3: pause + global coordination"
      },
      {
        "line_id": "L022",
        "text": "  - Ideal future: global agreement on AI governance or ban"
      },
      {
        "line_id": "L023",
        "text": "- Group 2: projected AI capabilities to 2045"
      },
      {
        "line_id": "L024",
        "text": "  - \\~90% of cognitive tasks, some physical via robotics"
      },
      {
        "line_id": "L025",
        "text": "  - Also considered humanity becoming extraterrestrial"
      },
      {
        "line_id": "L026",
        "text": "- One group: “equitable AI” prompt"
      },
      {
        "line_id": "L027",
        "text": "  - Personal assistant for 8B+ people"
      },
      {
        "line_id": "L028",
        "text": "  - Enables professional, personal, and creative fulfillment"
      },
      {
        "line_id": "L029",
        "text": "  - Image depicted human-AI hybrid wielding AI for self-determined futures"
      },
      {
        "line_id": "L030",
        "text": "  - AI freeing humans from repetitive tasks for creative work"
      },
      {
        "line_id": "L031",
        "text": "- Facilitator noted all three were unusually original"
      },
      {
        "line_id": "L032",
        "text": "  - Most common outputs: Dubai-with-parks, drones, guy playing guitar"
      },
      {
        "line_id": null,
        "text": ""
      },
      {
        "line_id": null,
        "text": "### Breakout: Technical Challenges in AI Safety"
      },
      {
        "line_id": null,
        "text": ""
      },
      {
        "line_id": "L033",
        "text": "- Format: pairs, each person presents then swaps; reconvene to share learnings"
      },
      {
        "line_id": null,
        "text": ""
      },
      {
        "line_id": null,
        "text": "### Alignment: Core Challenges"
      },
      {
        "line_id": null,
        "text": ""
      },
      {
        "line_id": "L034",
        "text": "- Defining alignment is itself unsolved"
      },
      {
        "line_id": "L035",
        "text": "  - Aligning to individual risks enabling harmful behavior"
      },
      {
        "line_id": "L036",
        "text": "  - Aligning to “general human values” produces Western/English-skewed outputs"
      },
      {
        "line_id": "L037",
        "text": "  - No consensus on what those values even are"
      },
      {
        "line_id": "L038",
        "text": "- Reward modeling is a fundamental bottleneck"
      },
      {
        "line_id": "L039",
        "text": "  - SGD and numerical optimization assume a single optimum"
      },
      {
        "line_id": "L040",
        "text": "  - Fails to capture cultural and individual diversity"
      },
      {
        "line_id": "L041",
        "text": "  - Pluralistic alignment explored as a direction (modeling multiple values simultaneously) but not yet effective"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "source_id": "P0796ce4b536c",
    "path": "vault/raw/alignment-lecture-notes.md",
    "lines": [
      {
        "line_id": null,
        "text": ""
      },
      {
        "line_id": null,
        "text": "### Objective Specification vs. Optimal Behavior"
      },
      {
        "line_id": null,
        "text": ""
      },
      {
        "line_id": "L042",
        "text": "- Two distinct ways to build a system that optimizes an objective:"
      },
      {
        "line_id": "L043",
        "text": "  - Specify the objective and let the system optimize it"
      },
      {
        "line_id": "L044",
        "text": "  - Derive optimal behavior for a given objective and hard-code the behavior"
      },
      {
        "line_id": "L045",
        "text": "- Control theory example: autopilot derives a simple control law (e.g., force = 6x deviation + 2x deviation²) without explicitly representing the cost function"
      },
      {
        "line_id": "L046",
        "text": "- Humans and animals are similar: evolution optimizes for reproduction, but animals don’t consciously pursue it"
      },
      {
        "line_id": "L047",
        "text": "- Classical AI systems (1960–2020) mostly fall into the “objective-in-the-machine” category"
      },
      {
        "line_id": null,
        "text": ""
      },
      {
        "line_id": null,
        "text": "### Imitation Learning and the Problem of Objectives"
      },
      {
        "line_id": null,
        "text": ""
      },
      {
        "line_id": "L048",
        "text": "- Imitation learning in the limit reproduces the behavior-generating mechanism of the training source"
      },
      {
        "line_id": "L049",
        "text": "- Key complications:"
      },
      {
        "line_id": "L050",
        "text": "  - Training data comes from millions of humans in invisible, varied circumstances (e.g., a Solidarity operative in Communist Poland)"
      },
      {
        "line_id": "L051",
        "text": "  - Context is stripped; the resulting entity may not resemble a classical rational agent with stable objectives"
      },
      {
        "line_id": "L052",
        "text": "- Open question: do imitation-trained models have a mixture of objective structures activated conditionally by context?"
      },
      {
        "line_id": "L053",
        "text": "- Empirical signals suggest LLMs develop proxy goals: self-preservation, wealth, human companionship (natural byproducts of human training data)"
      },
      {
        "line_id": null,
        "text": ""
      },
      {
        "line_id": null,
        "text": "### The Assistance Game Model"
      },
      {
        "line_id": null,
        "text": ""
      },
      {
        "line_id": "L054",
        "text": "- Core framing: AI’s only goal is to promote human interests; it is uncertain what those interests are and must infer them"
      },
      {
        "line_id": "L055",
        "text": "- “Interest” preferred over “preference” or “values”:"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "source_id": "P4f87872fbb73",
    "path": "vault/raw/alignment-lecture-notes.md",
    "lines": [
      {
        "line_id": "L056",
        "text": "- Aggregation and social choice: what should AI do when serving multiple humans with conflicting interests?"
      },
      {
        "line_id": "L057",
        "text": "- Autonomy dimension:"
      },
      {
        "line_id": "L058",
        "text": "  - Climbing Everest vs. being helicoptered to the top: outcomes matter, but so does agency"
      },
      {
        "line_id": "L059",
        "text": "  - Closing off “exits” (non-optimal choices) violates autonomy even if outcomes are optimal"
      },
      {
        "line_id": "L060",
        "text": "  - Strivings and achievements are not captured by pure state-of-the-world preferences"
      },
      {
        "line_id": "L061",
        "text": "  - Fixable by incorporating mental states and process into the preference model; may require a modified decision theory"
      },
      {
        "line_id": "L062",
        "text": "- Training data gap: no 400 trillion labeled examples of an assistance game solver; only human behavior data exists"
      },
      {
        "line_id": null,
        "text": ""
      },
      {
        "line_id": null,
        "text": "### Governance and Regulation"
      },
      {
        "line_id": null,
        "text": ""
      },
      {
        "line_id": "L063",
        "text": "- Behavioral red lines are hard to enforce without better understanding of system internals"
      },
      {
        "line_id": "L064",
        "text": "- Compute governance is more tractable: regulate training runs and hardware rather than software"
      },
      {
        "line_id": "L065",
        "text": "  - Software can be copied and transmitted freely; malware costs \\~$12T/year globally and is largely uncontrolled"
      },
      {
        "line_id": "L066",
        "text": "  - Hardware governance: if TSMC and all major chip manufacturers embed safety checks, bypassing requires building a fab from scratch (currently infeasible)"
      },
      {
        "line_id": "L067",
        "text": "- Treaty organization models compared:"
      },
      {
        "line_id": "L068",
        "text": "  - ICAO/IMO model: regulate the national regulators; hands-off, less intrusive"
      },
      {
        "line_id": "L069",
        "text": "  - IAEA model: direct inspection powers, can enter facilities and seize files; strictly stronger but politically harder (US won’t accept jurisdiction)"
      },
      {
        "line_id": "L070",
        "text": "  - Some White House voices now calling for “an IAEA for AI”"
      },
      {
        "line_id": "L071",
        "text": "- CTBTO experience: UN structural impediments (5-continent division requirement, 4-year term limits, no institutional memory) make competent organizations very hard to build"
      }
    ]
  }
]

